# popkern

> $tagline

popkern is an open-source parametric boundary-representation (BRep) CAD
kernel in Rust. Parts are short Rhai scripts that record a feature tree;
the same script builds the same part in the browser (popkern live) or
natively. Booleans, fillets and chamfers (with G2 corner caps), sweeps,
revolves, lofts, shelling, constrained sketches, assemblies and STEP in
and out. When an operation cannot produce a valid solid it declines with
a structured error instead of returning a broken one. License: AGPL-3.0
for the kernel, with an exception that leaves user scripts, models and
STEP files to their authors.

## Install

macOS and Linux:

```sh
curl -fsSL ${base}install.sh | sh
```

Windows (PowerShell):

```powershell
irm ${base}install.ps1 | iex
```

This installs the `popkern` command, which includes popkern live served
locally. Nothing to install to try it: ${base}live/ runs the kernel in
the browser (compiled to WebAssembly).

## Quick start

```sh
popkern new my-parts/bracket     # scaffold a project: popkern.toml + migrations/001_base.rhai
popkern serve my-parts           # popkern live on http://127.0.0.1:7690, projects from my-parts/
popkern run part.rhai --step part.step --png part.png   # one script straight to STEP and a render
popkern build my-parts/bracket --step bracket.step      # a project's migrations, in order, to STEP
```

A part script (a plate with a boss, rounded; from the scripting guide,
where every code block runs in the test suite):

```rhai
$example
```

`popkern run` exit codes: 0 served, 2 script error, 3 kernel decline,
4 gate or export failure, 5 project structure error, 6 diverged history
under `--strict`. `--json` prints the report as JSON.

A project is a folder of numbered migration scripts
(`migrations/001_plate.rhai`, `002_boss.rhai`, ...), each starting from
the previous one's model with `previous()`. Projects are plain files and
build to one `.pkn` document or a STEP file.

## Use popkern from an AI agent (MCP)

`popkern serve` also answers the Model Context Protocol at `/mcp`
(JSON-RPC over HTTP). For Claude Code:

```sh
claude mcp add --transport http popkern http://127.0.0.1:7690/mcp
```

Tools: `list_projects`, `read_project`, `create_project`, `write_file`
(a migration or `popkern.toml`), `delete_file`, `build_project` (outcome,
per-migration status, failures, parameters, and per body its face count,
bounds and watertightness), `run_script` (try a loose script without
saving) and `export_step`. The server binds to loopback only and has no
authentication.

## API and documentation

- [Scripting guide](${base}scripting-guide.md): the whole Rhai API on one page: mental model, planes, sketches, every feature verb, edge and face selectors, parameters, reading the model back, migrations and projects, error classes, assemblies, films, and a cheat-sheet. Read this first.
- [Scripting surface specification](https://github.com/popkern-dev/popkern/blob/main/docs/architecture/scripting-surface.md): the normative, language-agnostic contract behind the script API, MCP tools and GUI; every operation, parameter and error class, with stability marks.
- [Research citations](https://github.com/popkern-dev/popkern/blob/main/docs/reference/citations.md): which papers each kernel algorithm implements.
- [README](https://github.com/popkern-dev/popkern/blob/main/README.md): building from source.
- [AGENTS.md](https://github.com/popkern-dev/popkern/blob/main/AGENTS.md): the crate map, dependency rules and quality gates, for building on the kernel directly from Rust or contributing.

